  ],
  "text": "Augmented reality and virtual reality differ in how they treat the real world. Virtual reality creates a virtual environment that replaces the user's sense of reality and does not need a real object or place. Augmented or mixed reality requires an existing real place, and augmented reality can add digital content over the user's real-world perception."
}

{
  "points": [
    {
      "facts": [1],
      "point": "The reality-virtuality continuum is the scale from completely real to completely virtual environments."
    },
    {
      "facts": [2],
      "point": "Augmented reality is the intermediate state where a real environment is augmented with virtual content."
    },
    {
      "facts": [3],
      "point": "Augmented virtuality is the opposite intermediate state where a virtual environment is augmented with real-world content."
    },
    {
      "facts": [4],
      "point": "Mixed reality, broadly, is the entire space between the real and virtual environment extremes, with varying degrees of augmentation."
    }
  ],
  "text": "The reality-virtuality continuum gives a scale for where immersive experiences lie, with a completely real environment at one end and a completely virtual one at the other. Augmented reality and augmented virtuality are intermediate states, distinguished by whether virtual content is added to reality or real content is added to virtuality. Mixed reality, in a broad sense, covers the entire space between the extremes, so it includes those intermediate states."
}

