      "question": "How do interaural level difference and interaural time difference provide binaural cues for estimating the direction of a sound source?",
      "reference_answer": "They are binaural sound localization cues. Interaural level difference is the difference in sound magnitude heard by each ear, with the unobstructed ear receiving stronger sound when an acoustic shadow occurs. Interaural time difference comes from the sound reaching one ear before the other because the ears are about 21.5 centimeters apart and sound travels that distance in about 0.6 milliseconds. The level and timing differences can indicate the direction from which the sound is coming.",
