      "key_facts": [30, 31, 34],
      "question": "Why is wayfinding support essential in a complex virtual environment even when a system provides many locomotion techniques?",
      "reference_answer": "Wayfinding aims to build a mental model of the surroundings and of the path to a destination, so that the user can locate themselves and choose a suitable path. Environment design should support this mental map. Without wayfinding support, travel techniques cannot be used efficiently or effectively, and the overall user experience deteriorates.",
      "concepts": ["wayfinding", "mental model", "user experience"]
    },
    {
      "key_facts": [22, 23, 24],
      "question": "How does route planning-based locomotion create movement differently from target selection-based locomotion?",
      "reference_answer": "In target selection-based locomotion, the user selects a destination and the viewpoint changes to make the user feel moved there, as in teleportation or map dragging. In route planning-based locomotion, the user selects a route to the target, and different viewpoint changes along that route make the user feel movement along a path. This gives more granular control and slightly more natural movement than teleportation.",
      "concepts": ["target selection-based locomotion", "route planning-based locomotion", "viewpoint"]
    },
